Next.js & TypeScript Codebase Guidelines – “7.5” Standard
1. Project Structure & Code Organization
1.1. Pages & API Routes
Pages Directory:

Use the pages/ directory (or the new app/ directory in Next.js 13+) to define routes.
For pages using server-side rendering or static generation, structure your files to maximize clarity.
Separate page-level concerns from UI components. Keep pages primarily as composition layers (using functions like getStaticProps or getServerSideProps).
API Endpoints:

Place API routes in pages/api/ (or app/api/ for Next.js 13+).
Limit these files to handling HTTP-specific concerns: parsing requests, authentication checks, and delegating business logic to service functions.
Aim for concise files (ideal: 50–75 lines; max: 100 lines). If an API route grows too large, refactor business logic into services.
1.2. Components
UI Components:
Store React components in components/ or within feature-specific directories.
Server vs. Client Components:
When using Next.js 13+, clearly mark client components (using "use client") and server components.
Keep server components focused on data fetching and heavy computations, while client components manage interactivity.
Break down large components into smaller ones (ideal file length: 150–250 lines; max: 300 lines).
1.3. Services & Business Logic
Services Directory:
Place domain logic and external integrations in a dedicated lib/services/ or services/ folder.
Ensure these modules are independent, testable, and focused on a single concern.
Use TypeScript’s typing to enforce clear contracts between services and their consumers.
1.4. Utilities & Helpers
Utilities:
Organize generic helper functions in a utils/ directory.
Keep these functions pure and well-typed to guarantee predictable behavior.
Document any non-obvious business rules or transformations.
1.5. Types & Interfaces
Types Directory:
Maintain a centralized types/ folder where you define domain-specific interfaces, types, and enums.
Leverage TypeScript’s advanced types (unions, intersections, generics) to create self-documenting and reusable definitions.
Export common types from an index file to simplify imports across the codebase.
2. File Size, Complexity, and Modularity
2.1. File Length Guidelines
Pages & API Files:

Ideal: 50–75 lines; Max: 100 lines.
Keep logic minimal and delegate to services/utilities.
React Components:

Ideal: 150–250 lines; Max: 300 lines.
Use hooks and smaller sub-components to break down complex logic.
Services & Utilities:

Ideal: 150–250 lines; Max: 300 lines.
Refactor and split files that approach or exceed these limits.
2.2. Modularization & Refactoring
Regularly review files during code reviews.
If any module becomes too large or unwieldy, extract related functions or logic into separate files.
Prioritize separation of concerns to reduce duplication and improve testability.
3. Real-Time Data Handling & External Integrations
3.1. WebSocket & Real-Time Clients
Encapsulation:
Isolate WebSocket logic in dedicated service modules (e.g., services/realtime.ts).
Implement reconnection strategies (e.g., exponential backoff) uniformly across the project.
Validate incoming data with the types defined in your types/ directory.
3.2. External APIs & Data Fetching
Data Fetching in Next.js:
Utilize Next.js data fetching methods (getStaticProps, getServerSideProps, or React hooks in client components) to keep data access predictable.
Cache data and manage state effectively using SWR or React Query where appropriate.
4. Error Handling & Logging
4.1. Centralized Error Management
Error Boundaries:

Implement error boundaries in your React components to catch and handle UI errors gracefully.
Create custom error classes for domain-specific issues and use them consistently.
Logging:

Use structured logging with clear severity levels. Avoid leaving console.log statements in production code.
Consider integrating log aggregation services for monitoring (e.g., LogRocket, Sentry).
4.2. Consistent API Responses
Return structured JSON responses from API routes (e.g., { error: string, details?: any }).
Include HTTP status codes and clear error messages to aid in debugging and user feedback.
5. Data Transformation, Naming, and Performance
5.1. Data Transformation
Helper Functions:
Write explicit functions for transforming data. Document edge cases and performance implications.
Use memoization or caching for heavy computations when necessary.
5.2. Naming Conventions
Descriptive Naming:
Use clear, descriptive names for functions, components, and variables to reflect their purpose.
Maintain consistency across modules to reduce cognitive load for new developers.
5.3. Performance Considerations
Optimization:
Use Next.js optimizations like code-splitting and lazy loading.
Profile and optimize data fetching and rendering to ensure a smooth user experience.
6. Database & Prisma Integration
6.1. Data Access with Prisma
Centralized Database Module:
Use Prisma as your ORM and centralize its usage in a dedicated module (e.g., lib/db.ts).
Isolate direct queries in the service layer rather than in API routes or components.
6.2. Schema Management & Migrations
Schema Files & Migrations:
Keep your Prisma schema file updated and document migration changes.
Use version control for database schemas and test migrations in staging before deploying to production.
7. Documentation & Developer Onboarding
7.1. Project Documentation
README & Docs:
Provide a comprehensive README.md with setup instructions, architectural overviews, and development workflow guidelines.
Maintain additional documentation for API endpoints, services, and key utilities.
7.2. Inline Code Documentation
Doc Blocks & Comments:
Use TypeScript’s JSDoc annotations to document public functions, interfaces, and complex logic.
Focus on the “why” behind decisions rather than just the “what.”
8. Striving for a 7.5 Standard in Next.js/TypeScript
Consistency & Simplicity:
Emphasize clear, consistent code over clever tricks. Maintain simplicity for easier onboarding and collaboration.
Testability & Agility:
Write unit and integration tests for critical paths. Use tools like Jest and React Testing Library.
Continuous Improvement:
Encourage regular code reviews and refactoring sessions to keep technical debt minimal.
Type Safety:
Fully leverage TypeScript to catch errors early and document expected interfaces.